    description: 'Gow Crater is an impact crater in Saskatchewan, Canada. It is 5 kilometres (3 mi) in diameter and the age is estimated to be less than 250 million years (Triassic or later). The crater contains a classic crater lake (Gow Lake) with an island (Calder Island) formed by the central uplift. It is the smallest known crater in Canada with an uplift structure. The crater is unique in that it is the only preserved example of a transitional impact structure on Earth. This is characterized by a crater having relatively smooth walls and a floor partly or completely covered by debris slumped from the crater...\n\nAlternative Lore: According to alternative history theorists, Gow crater represents a ground-zero site of an ancient high-yield thermonuclear blast or particle-beam weapon strike predating recorded history. Proponents point to the extreme presence of shocked quartz and glass-like tektites as evidence of localized artificial vitrification rather than a natural kinetic impact.',
